==English==
{{was wotd|2006|October|1}}
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|cosmopolite|an}}. Compare {{m|en|metropolitan}} and {{m|en|megalopolitan}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌkɒz.məˈpɒl.ɪ.tən/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌkɑz.məˈpɑl.ɪ.tən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cosmopolitan.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-cosmopolitan.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[inclusive|Inclusive]]; affecting the whole [[world]].
# {{lb|en|of a place|or|institution}} Composed of [[people]] from all over the world.
# {{lb|en|of a person}} At [[ease]] in any part of the world; familiar with many [[culture]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-11-25|author=Caroline Ferguson|title=What kind of global citizens are teachers creating?|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/teacher-network/teacher-blog/2013/nov/25/global-citizenship-teachers-creating|passage=The philosophy of cosmopolitanism underlies theories of global citizenship. Cosmopolitanism is the ability to balance a local and global identity. A '''cosmopolitan''' individual engages meaningfully with different cultures and feels at home in the world.}}
# {{lb|en|biology|ecology}} [[growing|Growing]] or [[living]] in many parts of the world; widely distributed.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=David K. Yeates; Brian M. Wiegmann|title=The Evolutionary Biology of Flies|page=372
|passage=Domestication and the evolution of anthrophilic traits in houseflies and some drosophilids have allowed certain species to attain '''cosmopolitan''' status.}}

